 {
  "verse_id": "chunk-v2-r-astamo-vimarsa-v32",
  "song": "chunk-v2-r-astamo-vimarsa",
  "verse": 32,
  "plain_english": "Dyczkowski-mode.*\n\n**The great-Lordship: the freedom in the appearance-group**\n\nThe knower, with his powers of knowledge, memory, and removal, the inner-made-infinite-world-form, has\nbeen established. Now Abhinavagupta asks what makes him *maheśvara*, the great Lord. The great-Lordship\n(māheśvarya) is twofold: it is directed at objects of knowledge and at objects of action. Here, in the\njñānādhikāra, the knowledge-directed lordship is determined. What is the jñāna? It is the\nself-experience \"aham\" — the \"I\" — contracted by the tint of the self-splitting appearance. And the\nlordship is the *freedom in the appearance-group*: the freedom with which the appearances arise, vary,\nand depend on one another.\n\nThe doubt arises at once. \"All the dealing,\" the objector says, \"is in the param-eśvara alone. But the\ndealing's animator is one — there is nothing of its own beyond it. So what cause is there for the\nvaried dealing of the world, with all its clearness and variety?\" And kārikā 1 answers: the appearances\nare not all alike. Some are dependent on the eye's co-presence at that time — the \"this jar,\" raised by\nthe impression's strength, depending on the perception that is near, sensory — and these appear in the\nconstruction-dealing that follows immediately upon the perception. Others appear otherwise — in the\nblind, in the darkness, and the rest, where there is no such eye-co-presence. The appearances are not\na single flat mass; they have their own dependence, their own conditions, their own arising.\n\nAnd this is precisely the freedom. The varied dealing of the world is not caused by something outside\nthe Lord; it is the Lord's own freedom in the appearance-group — the freedom by which some appearances\ndepend on the senses, some do not, yet all are one. The appearance-group is not inert; it is the\nmanifestation of the Lord's freedom, the jñāna as the \"aham\" that shines through all the appearances,\nunified in the one knower.\n\nSo the aṣṭamo vimarśa opens the great-Lordship. The Lord is maheśvara because he is free in the\nappearance-group: the appearances arise, depend, and vary by his freedom, and yet they are one, because\nthey are all his own \"aham\"-manifestation. The knowledge-directed lordship is this freedom — the\nself-experience that runs through all the appearances, the one \"I\" in which the whole world of\nappearances rests. And this, Abhinavagupta will go on to show, grounds both the knowledge and the\naction — the full great-Lordship of the one who is free in all the appearances, the maheśvara.\n\n- **Preserved:** the maṅgala; the established knower; the māheśvarya twofold (knowledge/action);\n  the jñānaviṣaya here; the jñāna as the \"aham\"-experience contracted by the tint; the freedom in the\n  appearance-group; the 11-verse plan; the doubt (all dealing in the Lord; the animator one; no cause\n  for the variety); kārikā 1 (the appearances dependent on the eye's co-presence, only somewhere;\n  otherwise in the blind, the darkness); the \"kevala\" (only) distinction; the impression-raised \"this\n  jar\"; the construction-dealing following the perception.\n- **Added (SUPPLIED, recoverable):** \"the freedom with which the appearances arise, vary, and depend\n  on one another\" (the māheśvarya); \"it is the Lord's own freedom in the appearance-group\" (the\n  variety's cause). Interpretive unpacking, not new doctrine.\n- **NOT resolved:** the 11-verse plan's full mapping; the sphuṭatva-vicitra-vicāra doubt's detail.\n  Logged, not invented.\nEOF\necho \"V2R L2: $(wc -l < /mnt/HC_Volume_106427611/sanskritree/translations/_stack/ipvv/pilot/pilot_V2R_L2_read.md) lines\"",
